---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/Lesson.astro';
import Quiz from '../../components/Quiz.astro';
import SimplexSim from '../../components/sims/SimplexSim.astro';

const papers = [
  {
    title: 'Simplex Consensus: A Simple and Fast Consensus Protocol',
    authors: 'Benjamin Y. Chan & Rafael Pass',
    year: '2023',
    href: 'https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/463',
    note: 'the original paper',
  },
  {
    title: 'commonware-consensus: simplex',
    authors: 'Commonware',
    year: 'ongoing',
    href: 'https://docs.rs/commonware-consensus/latest/commonware_consensus/simplex/index.html',
    note: 'a production implementation, with a precise spec of each view',
  },
  {
    title: 'HotStuff: BFT Consensus in the Lens of Blockchain',
    authors: 'Maofan Yin, Dahlia Malkhi, Michael K. Reiter, Guy Golan Gueta & Ittai Abraham',
    year: '2018',
    href: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.05069',
    note: 'the protocol Simplex is most often compared against',
  },
];
---

<Lesson
  id="spx"
  dek="A BFT protocol simple enough to prove on a whiteboard, which still matches the best known latency. Commonware’s consensus library is built on it."
  before={['Tendermint', 'HotStuff']}
  papers={papers}
>
  <SimplexSim slot="sim" />

  <h2>The problem it solved</h2>
  <p>
    Classical BFT protocols have two modes. The <em>normal path</em> runs while the leader is honest. The
    <em>view change</em> is a separate protocol for replacing a bad leader, and it has to carry enough state across the
    switch to stay safe. PBFT's view change is famously intricate, and it's where many subtle bugs in BFT systems
    live. Tendermint's locks and HotStuff's pacemaker solve the same problem, in different ways.
  </p>
  <p>
    Simplex, by Benjamin Chan and Rafael Pass, removes the second mode. A bad leader is handled by the same few rules
    as a good one.
  </p>

  <h2>How a view works</h2>
  <p>Time is split into numbered <strong>views</strong>, each with one leader. In each view:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The leader proposes a block that extends the chain it has seen certified.</li>
    <li>
      Validators who receive a valid proposal in time vote to <strong>notarize</strong> it. If their timer expires
      first, they vote to <strong>nullify</strong> the view instead.
    </li>
    <li>
      Votes from 2/3 of validators form a certificate: a <em>notarization</em> for the block, or a
      <em>nullification</em> for the view. Either one moves everyone to the next view.
    </li>
    <li>
      A validator that saw the block notarized and did not vote to nullify also sends a <strong>finalize</strong> vote.
      2/3 finalize votes make the block final, along with everything it builds on.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    That's the whole protocol. There is no separate recovery mode. A view with a dead leader simply ends in a
    nullification, and the next leader builds on the last notarized block.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it's safe</h2>
  <p>
    An honest validator never sends both a finalize vote and a nullify vote for the same view. Suppose a block is
    finalized in view <em>v</em>: 2/3 sent finalize votes. A nullification of <em>v</em> would need another 2/3 sending
    nullify votes. Those two groups overlap in at least one honest validator, who would have had to send both. So once
    a block is final, its view can never be nullified, and every later leader has to build on it.
  </p>
  <p>The proof in the paper is about a page long, which is a big part of why Simplex caught on.</p>

  <h2>Why it's fast</h2>
  <p>
    Notice that in the animation, the next view starts as soon as a block is <em>notarized</em>, without waiting for it
    to be final. Finalization catches up one step behind. This gives Simplex two good numbers:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Block time of two network hops:</strong> the proposal goes out, and the votes come back.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Finality in three hops:</strong> one more round of finalize votes. That matches the best possible latency
      for a partially synchronous protocol that tolerates a third of validators being faulty.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>In production: Commonware</h2>
  <p>
    Commonware's <code>simplex</code> engine follows the paper with practical changes. It uses separate
    <code>notarize</code> and <code>nullify</code> messages rather than a vote for a "dummy block". It adds a leader
    timeout that skips leaders who have been offline recently. It can aggregate votes with BLS threshold signatures,
    so a certificate stays the same size however many validators sign. It also lets the application
    <em>certify</em> a notarized block before finalizing, for example after checking that erasure-coded pieces of the
    block arrived.
  </p>
  <p>
    That production experience led directly to the next lesson: Minimmit asks whether the finalize round can be removed
    entirely.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="side">
    <div class="box">
      <h3>How it compares</h3>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Next block after</th><th>Bad leader</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Tendermint</td><td>commit (2 vote rounds)</td><td>new round; locks carry safety</td></tr>
          <tr><td>HotStuff</td><td>1 phase, pipelined</td><td>pacemaker</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Simplex</td><td>notarization (1 vote round)</td><td>nullify, same path</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Quiz
      question="In view 4 the leader never sends a block. What do validators do?"
      options={[
        { text: 'Wait until the leader comes back' },
        { text: 'Time out, vote to nullify the view, and move on', correct: true },
        { text: 'Run a separate view-change protocol to pick a new leader' },
      ]}
      right="Right. A nullification is just another certificate, so a bad leader costs one timeout and nothing else."
      wrong="Not quite. Simplex has no separate recovery mode: validators nullify the view and the next leader takes over."
    />
    <Quiz
      question="Why can view 5 start before view 4’s block is final?"
      options={[
        { text: 'A notarization proves 2/3 accepted the block, which is enough to build on; finality follows a step later', correct: true },
        { text: 'Because Simplex never finalizes blocks' },
        { text: 'Because leaders are trusted to propose correct blocks' },
      ]}
      right="Right. Building on notarized blocks keeps blocks coming every two hops, while finalize votes trail one step behind."
      wrong="Not quite. Simplex does finalize, and it never trusts the leader. A notarization certificate is what makes it safe to build on."
    />
  </Fragment>
</Lesson>
